    {
      "id": "ituYJj5hdFK0VHwybOCfi",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 289.4605348606358,
      "y": 1934.1110276277495,
      "width": 942.7627873861902,
      "height": 633.0000518743959,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#eebefa",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b07",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 82440605,
      "version": 3592,
      "versionNonce": 375616308,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1782585710051,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) Edits \n- Every time someone makes an edit we can't update the s3. S3 is immutable so every update is\nbasically deletion and creation of a new document. This is computationally expensive. \n- We store the edits in our database. Whenever someone fetches the document for the first time,\nwe return the latest version from S3 + updates from the database. \n- After certain duration we can CDC changes from the database to S3. This is essentially the\nsolution for edits and version history. \n\n2) Scaling web socket services\n- they are stateful hence all the clients for a doc needs to be connected to same instance\n- we use consistent hashing for this based on the doc id. \n\n3) We can add CDN for delivering the documents. Toomstoning for deleting stuff. \n\n4) OT vs CRDTS\n-  Operational Transformation (OT)\n    > A central service orders edits and rewrites (transforms) them so user intent is preserved.\n    > Lighter clients\n    > Requires a sequencer and server-side transformations\n- Conflict-free Replicated Data Types (CRDTs)\n    > Each edit carries metadata so replicas can merge without a central order.\n    > Great for offline-first collaboration\n    > More complex data structures and larger metadata\n- With a centralized collaboration service and online-first workflow, OT fits better. It keeps client\nlogic lighter and uses the ordering point we already operate. If offline-first becomes a primary\nrequirement, CRDTs become more attractive despite their complexity.",
      "fontSize": 19.47692467305834,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) Edits \n- Every time someone makes an edit we can't update the s3. S3 is immutable so every update is basically deletion and creation of a new document. This is computationally expensive. \n- We store the edits in our database. Whenever someone fetches the document for the first time, we return the latest version from S3 + updates from the database. \n- After certain duration we can CDC changes from the database to S3. This is essentially the solution for edits and version history. \n\n2) Scaling web socket services\n- they are stateful hence all the clients for a doc needs to be connected to same instance\n- we use consistent hashing for this based on the doc id. \n\n3) We can add CDN for delivering the documents. Toomstoning for deleting stuff. \n\n4) OT vs CRDTS\n-  Operational Transformation (OT)\n    > A central service orders edits and rewrites (transforms) them so user intent is preserved.\n    > Lighter clients\n    > Requires a sequencer and server-side transformations\n- Conflict-free Replicated Data Types (CRDTs)\n    > Each edit carries metadata so replicas can merge without a central order.\n    > Great for offline-first collaboration\n    > More complex data structures and larger metadata\n- With a centralized collaboration service and online-first workflow, OT fits better. It keeps client logic lighter and uses the ordering point we already operate. If offline-first becomes a primary requirement, CRDTs become more attractive despite their complexity.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "HSqc5H8eZJNjarL9VIP-z",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 290.4690811831813,
      "y": -363.9708229089583,
      "width": 916.2693684895834,
      "height": 2200,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0I",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1106172592,
      "version": 11290,
      "versionNonce": 1409215796,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1782577000730,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "\n1) Functional Requirements:  \n- User should be able to create, open, update, and delete their own documents.\n- stream live edits to collaborators in near real-time.\n- must merge simultaneous edits to converge to the same final document state.\n- show collaborators' live cursors and text selections.\n- User should be able to share documents and control access levels.\n- User should be able to view previous revisions and restore to an earlier version.\n\n\n2) Scale Understanding \n- DAU: 1 Million.\n- Traffic spike: 5x during peak hours.\n- Read/Write ratio: 10:1.\n- Average document size: 100KB.\n- Average documents per user: 10.\n- Edit frequency: 1 edit per second per active document during peak.\n- Concurrency limit: Up to 100 concurrent users per document.\n\n2) Non Functional Requirements (SPARCS):  \n- Scalability: Support up to 100 concurrent users per document and handle 5x peak traffic\nspikes.\n- Performance (Latency, Throughput): Low-latency edit/cursor updates (immediate feel),\nimmediate read-your-writes.\n- Availability: High availability during peak traffic and partial outages.\n- Reliability (Durability): High durability to prevent loss of document content and revision\nhistory.\n- Consistency: Edits must eventually converge so all replicas display the same final state.\n- Security: Strict permission enforcement on every read and write.\n\n3) BOE Calculations \n- QPS\n    > Read QPS : 10*(1M transactions)/ (10^5) => 100 QPS\n    > Write QPS : 1*(1M transactions)/ (10^5) => 10 QPS\n    > Peak QPS ≈ X5 \n\n- Storage \n    > Daily storage = 1M writes × 100KB x 10 = 1 TB\n    > Yearly storage = 365TB\n    > Total storage = 365TB*5 =1.825 PB \n    > replication = 3X \n\n4) Core Entities\n- User\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n5) API Design \n\nPOST /api/documents\n    Input: {title}\n    Output: 201 Created, {documentId, title}\n\nGET /api/documents/{documentId}\n    Output: 200 OK, {documentId, title, headRevisionId, content}\n\nPOST /api/documents/{documentId}/operations\n    Input: {baseRevisionId, operations: [{type, position, text}]}\n    Output: 202 Accepted, {status}\n\nGET /api/documents/{documentId}/history\n    Output: 200 OK, {revisions: [{revisionId, authorId, createdAt}]}\n\nPOST /api/documents/{documentId}/permissions\n    Input: {principalId, role}\n    Output: 200 OK, {status}\n\nGET (WebSocket) /api/documents/{documentId}/collaborate\n    Input (Client -> Server): {type, baseRevisionId, operation: {type, position, text}}\n    Output (Server -> Client): {type, revisionId, operation: {type, position, text}}",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "\n1) Functional Requirements:  \n- User should be able to create, open, update, and delete their own documents.\n- stream live edits to collaborators in near real-time.\n- must merge simultaneous edits to converge to the same final document state.\n- show collaborators' live cursors and text selections.\n- User should be able to share documents and control access levels.\n- User should be able to view previous revisions and restore to an earlier version.\n\n\n2) Scale Understanding \n- DAU: 1 Million.\n- Traffic spike: 5x during peak hours.\n- Read/Write ratio: 10:1.\n- Average document size: 100KB.\n- Average documents per user: 10.\n- Edit frequency: 1 edit per second per active document during peak.\n- Concurrency limit: Up to 100 concurrent users per document.\n\n2) Non Functional Requirements (SPARCS):  \n- Scalability: Support up to 100 concurrent users per document and handle 5x peak traffic spikes.\n- Performance (Latency, Throughput): Low-latency edit/cursor updates (immediate feel), immediate read-your-writes.\n- Availability: High availability during peak traffic and partial outages.\n- Reliability (Durability): High durability to prevent loss of document content and revision history.\n- Consistency: Edits must eventually converge so all replicas display the same final state.\n- Security: Strict permission enforcement on every read and write.\n\n3) BOE Calculations \n- QPS\n    > Read QPS : 10*(1M transactions)/ (10^5) => 100 QPS\n    > Write QPS : 1*(1M transactions)/ (10^5) => 10 QPS\n    > Peak QPS ≈ X5 \n\n- Storage \n    > Daily storage = 1M writes × 100KB x 10 = 1 TB\n    > Yearly storage = 365TB\n    > Total storage = 365TB*5 =1.825 PB \n    > replication = 3X \n\n4) Core Entities\n- User\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n5) API Design \n\nPOST /api/documents\n    Input: {title}\n    Output: 201 Created, {documentId, title}\n\nGET /api/documents/{documentId}\n    Output: 200 OK, {documentId, title, headRevisionId, content}\n\nPOST /api/documents/{documentId}/operations\n    Input: {baseRevisionId, operations: [{type, position, text}]}\n    Output: 202 Accepted, {status}\n\nGET /api/documents/{documentId}/history\n    Output: 200 OK, {revisions: [{revisionId, authorId, createdAt}]}\n\nPOST /api/documents/{documentId}/permissions\n    Input: {principalId, role}\n    Output: 200 OK, {status}\n\nGET (WebSocket) /api/documents/{documentId}/collaborate\n    Input (Client -> Server): {type, baseRevisionId, operation: {type, position, text}}\n    Output (Server -> Client): {type, revisionId, operation: {type, position, text}}",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
