      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) What Are RSS Feeds?\n- Imagine every news website had a standardized notice board where they automatically post\na short summary every time they publish a new story. Instead of you visiting 10,000\nwebsites to check for news, each site keeps its own tidy list that any program can read.\n- That \"standardized notice board\" is called an RSS feed (RSS stands for Really Simple\nSyndication).\n- An RSS feed is just a special file (written in a format called XML) that lists recent articles.\nAn RSS feed is designed for computers to read — it's plain, structured, and predictable.\n\n2) Crawling and Polling\n- Polling = regularly re-checking each RSS feed (e.g., \"every 5 minutes, look for new items\").\nSimple and effective.\n- Crawling = a program that visits webpages and reads their content directly. Used when a\npublisher doesn't offer an RSS feed.\n- Be respectful: good crawlers obey robots.txt (a file where sites say what's allowed) and\ndon't hammer servers too fast. Learn more from Google's crawling documentation.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) What Are RSS Feeds?\n- Imagine every news website had a standardized notice board where they automatically post a short summary every time they publish a new story. Instead of you visiting 10,000 websites to check for news, each site keeps its own tidy list that any program can read.\n- That \"standardized notice board\" is called an RSS feed (RSS stands for Really Simple Syndication).\n- An RSS feed is just a special file (written in a format called XML) that lists recent articles. An RSS feed is designed for computers to read — it's plain, structured, and predictable.\n\n2) Crawling and Polling\n- Polling = regularly re-checking each RSS feed (e.g., \"every 5 minutes, look for new items\"). Simple and effective.\n- Crawling = a program that visits webpages and reads their content directly. Used when a publisher doesn't offer an RSS feed.\n- Be respectful: good crawlers obey robots.txt (a file where sites say what's allowed) and don't hammer servers too fast. Learn more from Google's crawling documentation.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "2Or6gXU876BwqjoLWgqYw",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1247.2881769989274,
      "y": -346.5324356289584,
      "width": 916.2693684895834,
      "height": 3050,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bQ5",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 249337693,
      "version": 12042,
      "versionNonce": 1843718156,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1782576988020,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) Single User Editing - CRUD \n- We store metadata in a DB and body for the document in Object storage like S3\n\n2) Live Edit Streaming \n\nPUSHING EDITS \n- Bad Solution: Polling \n    > Clients request updates on a fixed schedule.\n    > Simple to implement\n    > Limitation: wasted requests when there are no edits, and updates feel delayed\n\n- Better Solution: SSE \n    > The server pushes updates over a long-lived HTTP connection.\n    > Better than polling for one way updates \n    > Limitation: clients still need a separate channel to send edits back\n\n- Great Solution: Websocket\n    >  bidirectional, long-lived connection carries edits in both directions.\n    > Near real-time delivery for reads and writes\n    > One channel for edits, acks, and presence updates\n\nFANOUT EDITS \n- Bad Solution\n    > The gateway broadcasts updates to every connected client.\n    > Great for a single gateway instance\n    > Limitation: once we scale gateways horizontally, collaborators on other gateways won't\nreceive updates\n\n- Great Solution: Pub-Sub\n    > The collaboration pipeline publishes each confirmed edit to a pub/sub system, and every\ngateway subscribed to that document relays it to its clients.\n    > Works across many gateways\n    > Buffers bursts so a slow gateway does not block others\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n3) Concurrent Edit Convergence \n- Bad Solution: Last-Write-Wins\n    > The server accepts the edit with the latest timestamp and drops the other.\n    > Simple to implement\n    > Limitation: users lose edits without warning\n\n- Great Solution: Operational Transformation (OT)\n    > The server rewrites concurrent operations so each user's intent is preserved.\n    > Preserves intent for most text edits\n    > Requires a central ordering point\n\n- Great Solution: Conflict-free Replicated Data Types (CRDTs)\n    > Clients attach metadata to each edit so replicas can merge without central\ncoordination.\n    > Great for offline-first collaboration\n    > More complex metadata and client logic\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n4) Cursor & Selection Presence\n- Normal Solution: Persist Cursor State in the Database\n    > Write each cursor update to the same database as document metadata.\n    > Simple consistency model\n    > Limitation: too much write traffic for short-lived data\n\n- Good Solution: In memory Store\n    > Keep cursor state in an in-memory store with auto-expiration (TTL, or time-to-live\nafter inactivity).\n    > Optimized for frequent updates\n    > Safe to drop during overload because it does not affect correctness\n    > Clients send presence_update events through the Real-time Gateway to the Presence\nService, which updates an in-memory store and broadcasts the latest cursor state to all\ncollaborators.\n\n\n5) Sharing & Permissions \n- Neutral Solution: Store Permissions Inside the Document Record\n    > Embed all ACLs (access control lists) directly in the document metadata.\n    > Simple for small documents\n    > Limitation: large permission lists make document reads heavier\n\n- Good Solution: Dedicated Access Control Service \n- Store permissions in a separate service optimized for access checks.\n- Scales independently from document metadata\n- Enables link-based sharing and audits\n\n\n6) Version History\n- Bad Solution : Store Full Snapshots Only\n    > Save the entire document every time a revision is created.\n    > Simple to restore\n    > Limitation: storage grows quickly for frequent edits\n\n- Good Solution: Store Deltas Only\n    > Store only the operations needed to reconstruct a revision (deltas, or change-only\nrecords).\n    > Storage efficient\n    > Limitation: reconstructing old versions can be slow\n\n- Great Solution: Hybrid Snapshots + Deltas\n    > Store periodic snapshots and deltas in between.\n    > Balanced storage and restore speed\n    > Common approach for large collaborative docs",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) Single User Editing - CRUD \n- We store metadata in a DB and body for the document in Object storage like S3\n\n2) Live Edit Streaming \n\nPUSHING EDITS \n- Bad Solution: Polling \n    > Clients request updates on a fixed schedule.\n    > Simple to implement\n    > Limitation: wasted requests when there are no edits, and updates feel delayed\n\n- Better Solution: SSE \n    > The server pushes updates over a long-lived HTTP connection.\n    > Better than polling for one way updates \n    > Limitation: clients still need a separate channel to send edits back\n\n- Great Solution: Websocket\n    >  bidirectional, long-lived connection carries edits in both directions.\n    > Near real-time delivery for reads and writes\n    > One channel for edits, acks, and presence updates\n\nFANOUT EDITS \n- Bad Solution\n    > The gateway broadcasts updates to every connected client.\n    > Great for a single gateway instance\n    > Limitation: once we scale gateways horizontally, collaborators on other gateways won't receive updates\n\n- Great Solution: Pub-Sub\n    > The collaboration pipeline publishes each confirmed edit to a pub/sub system, and every gateway subscribed to that document relays it to its clients.\n    > Works across many gateways\n    > Buffers bursts so a slow gateway does not block others\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n3) Concurrent Edit Convergence \n- Bad Solution: Last-Write-Wins\n    > The server accepts the edit with the latest timestamp and drops the other.\n    > Simple to implement\n    > Limitation: users lose edits without warning\n\n- Great Solution: Operational Transformation (OT)\n    > The server rewrites concurrent operations so each user's intent is preserved.\n    > Preserves intent for most text edits\n    > Requires a central ordering point\n\n- Great Solution: Conflict-free Replicated Data Types (CRDTs)\n    > Clients attach metadata to each edit so replicas can merge without central coordination.\n    > Great for offline-first collaboration\n    > More complex metadata and client logic\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n4) Cursor & Selection Presence\n- Normal Solution: Persist Cursor State in the Database\n    > Write each cursor update to the same database as document metadata.\n    > Simple consistency model\n    > Limitation: too much write traffic for short-lived data\n\n- Good Solution: In memory Store\n    > Keep cursor state in an in-memory store with auto-expiration (TTL, or time-to-live after inactivity).\n    > Optimized for frequent updates\n    > Safe to drop during overload because it does not affect correctness\n    > Clients send presence_update events through the Real-time Gateway to the Presence Service, which updates an in-memory store and broadcasts the latest cursor state to all collaborators.\n\n\n5) Sharing & Permissions \n- Neutral Solution: Store Permissions Inside the Document Record\n    > Embed all ACLs (access control lists) directly in the document metadata.\n    > Simple for small documents\n    > Limitation: large permission lists make document reads heavier\n\n- Good Solution: Dedicated Access Control Service \n- Store permissions in a separate service optimized for access checks.\n- Scales independently from document metadata\n- Enables link-based sharing and audits\n\n\n6) Version History\n- Bad Solution : Store Full Snapshots Only\n    > Save the entire document every time a revision is created.\n    > Simple to restore\n    > Limitation: storage grows quickly for frequent edits\n\n- Good Solution: Store Deltas Only\n    > Store only the operations needed to reconstruct a revision (deltas, or change-only records).\n    > Storage efficient\n    > Limitation: reconstructing old versions can be slow\n\n- Great Solution: Hybrid Snapshots + Deltas\n    > Store periodic snapshots and deltas in between.\n    > Balanced storage and restore speed\n    > Common approach for large collaborative docs",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "DQyIsz7cSPhloijNQ88Rx",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1252.8866386182547,
      "y": -389.28282291324217,
      "width": 507.6120300292969,
      "height": 35,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#e03131",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
